  "passages": [
    {
      "id": "pcp-short-abstract",
      "section": "Abstract",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "The PCP theorem states that every language in NP has a polynomial-time verifier with logarithmic randomness, constantly many proof-bit queries, perfect completeness and constant soundness error.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.np_subset_pcp"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "Abstract",
        "block": 2
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pcp-short-theorem",
      "section": "1",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "If $x\\in L$, there is one proof accepted for every random seed. If $x\\notin L$, every proof is accepted with probability at most $1/2$.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.np_subset_pcp"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "1. The theorem and the encoding problem",
        "block": 4
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pcp-short-colour-constraints",
      "section": "2",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "An honest colouring makes $A_P$ vanish on $H^m$ and $B_P$ vanish on $H^{2m}$. Conversely, these vanishing conditions recover a proper colouring: the first restricts every vertex to the three allowed values, and the second separates the values across each edge.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.Algebraic.ThreeColorGrid.colorable_iff_exists_constraints"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "2. Three-colouring as two vanishing conditions",
        "block": 7
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pcp-short-weighted-test",
      "section": "4",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "If (10) rejects with probability $\\delta<1/[2(c+2)^2]$, then $f$ is within $2\\delta$ of a degree-$c$ polynomial.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.Algebraic.GeneratorLowDegreeTest.exists_close"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "4. The local tests and their decoding guarantee",
        "block": 7
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pcp-short-binary-decoding",
      "section": "4",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "There is no small-rejection premise in (11), and both bounds concern the same decoded polynomial.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.Algebraic.BinaryLocalRowDecoding.exists_polynomial_distance_le"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "4. The local tests and their decoding guarantee",
        "block": 23
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pcp-short-outer-decoding",
      "section": "4",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "The restricted conclusion is for homogeneous linear labels.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.Algebraic.EncodedLineLowDegreeTest.exists_close"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "4. The local tests and their decoding guarantee",
        "block": 38
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pcp-short-outer-correction",
      "section": "5",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "This statement allows an arbitrary auxiliary table. Such a table may cause frequent rejection; (14) says that a corrector which seldom rejects cannot often return the wrong bit.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.Algebraic.BinaryLocalCorrection.failure_le"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "5. Correcting the nonuniform certificate queries",
        "block": 10
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pcp-short-zero",
      "section": "5",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "Thus small observed rejection forces the decoded polynomial to vanish on the grid, once the error terms are below the left side.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.Algebraic.BinaryZeroOnGridCorrection.rejectionRate_lower_bound"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "5. Correcting the nonuniform certificate queries",
        "block": 17
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "linked-source-12",
      "section": "6. One decoding for all seven checks",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "For completeness, start with a proper colouring, its exact interpolants and exact certificates, and the honest encoded line restrictions. If their degrees are below $b^c$, every component test accepts for every seed. This supplies one fixed eight-table proof with perfect completeness.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.Algebraic.ThreeColorVerifier.exists_proof_test_eq_true"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "6. One decoding for all seven checks",
        "block": 15
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "linked-source-13",
      "section": "7. Resources, implementation and the reverse inclusion",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "Repeating independently a constant number of times against the same fixed proof reduces soundness error to at most $1/2$, preserving logarithmic randomness and constant query complexity.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.Algebraic.ThreeColorAmplifiedVerifier.verification_guarantees"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "7. Resources, implementation and the reverse inclusion",
        "block": 12
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pcp-short-complexity",
      "section": "7",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "The operations behind these queries are polynomial-time computations.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.Lang.ThreeColorRuntime.polyTimeAlg"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "7. Resources, implementation and the reverse inclusion",
        "block": 15
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "linked-source-14",
      "section": "7. Resources, implementation and the reverse inclusion",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "For the converse, guess the first $p(n)$ proof bits and simulate the verifier on every random string. There are at most $2^b(2(n+2))^a$ such strings, and each simulation takes polynomial time. Perfect completeness gives a prefix for which every run accepts. If a prefix were accepted for a no-instance, its zero extension would be a fixed proof with acceptance probability one, contradicting soundness. Thus this finite witness relation is an NP relation, completing the equality in the theorem.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.pcp_subset_np_concrete"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "7. Resources, implementation and the reverse inclusion",
        "block": 19
      }
    },
